(Description)
Given an input string s and a pattern p, implement regular expression matching with support for '.' and '*' where:
- '.' Matches any single character.​​​​ 
- '*' Matches zero or more of the preceding element.

Return a boolean indicating whether the matching covers the entire input string (not partial).

--- Solution ---
class Solution {
    public boolean isMatch(String s, String p) {
        return backTrack(s, p, 0, 0);
    }

    private boolean backTrack(String s, String p, int sIndex, int pIndex) {
        if (pIndex == p.length()) {
            return sIndex == s.length();
        }

        boolean firstMatch =
                sIndex < s.length() &&
                (s.charAt(sIndex) == p.charAt(pIndex)
                || p.charAt(pIndex) == '.');

        // Next pattern character is '*'
        if (pIndex + 1 < p.length()
                && p.charAt(pIndex + 1) == '*') {

            return backTrack(s, p, sIndex, pIndex + 2) 
                || (firstMatch && backTrack(s, p, sIndex + 1, pIndex));
        }

        if (firstMatch) {
            return backTrack(s, p, sIndex + 1, pIndex + 1);
        }

        return false;
    }
}

--- Approach ---
The key concpet of this algorithm is the recursion, having the given two strings & managing them throughout the values of index.

In the recursion, there are three cases.
1. The characters are matched and next character is not *.
2. The characters are matched and next character at the string p is *.
3. The characters are not matched, but the next character at the string p is *.

At the first case, it calls the recursion after adding 1 for each index values.
Or at the case 2 and 3, it calls the both cases (skipping *, which means use the previous element zero, or adding only one for the string s, meaning it used previous element once).
Then, it uses or operator to check if one case could return true or not.

Becaue, if it returns true, that pattern that used * times at that case was mathing with the given s String.
